{"parentUuid":"e4a3031a-c846-40be-b85b-0eafe4b0b6e3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a67cf942cd679915d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0178oKnCrycoRj1D3yW4oLqc","type":"tool_result","content":"Based on the content provided, the post does not address any of those specific topics:\n\n1. **Propagation strength**: The article makes no mention of propagation strength comparisons between sequential counters, totalizers, sorting networks, or commander encodings.\n\n2. **Arc-consistency properties**: There is no discussion of arc-consistency versus non-arc-consistency characteristics of different encoding types.\n\n3. **Recommendations for CDCL solvers**: While the post discusses modern SAT solvers, it does not recommend specific encodings based on CDCL solver properties.\n\n4. **Benchmark comparisons**: The experimental evaluation in Figure 8 compares three variable *ordering* strategies (Natural, Random5, and Proximity) on MaxSAT problems, not different encoding types. The results measure \"number of solved formulas over time,\" not search tree size or detailed performance metrics between encoding methods themselves.\n\nThe post focuses primarily on how variable reordering within a single encoding type (totalizer) can improve solver performance, rather than comparing different encoding architectures or their theoretical properties."}]},"uuid":"b2243ab5-8890-4613-8180-6e4cfd8952f3","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:47.458Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e4a3031a-c846-40be-b85b-0eafe4b0b6e3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
