{"parentUuid":"fa13bcd5-56b8-4e8b-9f3d-641bf4681dd3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a67cf942cd679915d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CpDc8zzFC5PtnLJcHbiFeJ","type":"tool_result","content":"604:        model.Add(d_lo_var == int(shape[\"d_lo\"])).OnlyEnforceIf(lit_var)\n605:        model.Add(d_hi_var == int(shape[\"d_hi\"])).OnlyEnforceIf(lit_var)\n629:        model.Add(d_lo_var <= d_hi_var).OnlyEnforceIf(lit_var)\n638:            model.AddAllowedAssignments([d_lo_var, d_hi_var], fallback_rows).OnlyEnforceIf(\n662:    model.Add(d_lo_var >= int(d_lo_min)).OnlyEnforceIf(lit_var)\n663:    model.Add(d_lo_var <= int(d_lo_max)).OnlyEnforceIf(lit_var)\n664:    model.Add(d_hi_var >= int(d_hi_min)).OnlyEnforceIf(lit_var)\n665:    model.Add(d_hi_var <= int(d_hi_max)).OnlyEnforceIf(lit_var)\n2292:                ).OnlyEnforceIf(slot.active)\n2306:        self.model.Add(slot.mode == int(region.mode_id)).OnlyEnforceIf(lit)\n\n[Showing results with pagination = limit: 10]"}]},"uuid":"13f3a8f2-568b-40ad-b10d-7c7ba0a92b3c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:55.256Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fa13bcd5-56b8-4e8b-9f3d-641bf4681dd3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"f6789a78-430a-4647-90d2-658ec1803ed9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a67cf942cd679915d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WgsE8ehsysA5ULSssH8aAP","type":"tool_result","content":"# Empirical Results on Cardinality Constraint Encodings\n\n## Main Findings\n\nBased on extensive testing with the sat13 solver on combinatorial problems (grid squares and triangular lattices), the research compared three primary encodings:\n\n**Winner by Problem Type:**\n- **Sequential-counter encoding**: Fastest overall, particularly on larger problems requiring >10^8 memory accesses (18 of 21 cases)\n- **Tree-based encoding**: Second-best, uses substantially fewer auxiliary variables\n- **Sort-based encoding**: Slowest across nearly all test cases\n\n## Key Quote on Encoding Performance\n\n\"The sequential-counter encoding is found to be the fastest of these for a range of related, combinatorial test cases.\"\n\n## Propagation Strength Claims\n\nThe research developed \"strengthened\" variants adding constraints to reduce auxiliary variable solutions. Counterintuitively:\n\n\"These variants are found to have remarkably little effect on solution time, even when the number of clauses is approximately doubled.\"\n\nThis held across all three encodings, suggesting that \"unit propagation\" benefits are minimal despite reducing solution space by orders of magnitude.\n\n## Critical Finding on Size vs. Difficulty\n\n\"Clause count and other measures of encoding size are not reliable indicators of the difficulty of a SAT problem.\"\n\nThe fastest method (sequential-counter) paradoxically had the largest clause count, directly contradicting assumptions in optimization literature.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nThe research emphasizes that **no single encoding dominates universally**, concluding: \"testing is required to select the best encoding method for a specific problem.\""}]},"uuid":"d0792515-1ff1-4334-b252-6d49c8e81a53","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:27:01.261Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f6789a78-430a-4647-90d2-658ec1803ed9","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
